{"name":"Black Onyx","hex":"#2a272c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"black","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Absorbing all light and reflecting none; dark and hueless.","Without light."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour/color perceived in the absence of light, but also when no light is reflected, but rather absorbed.","A black dye or pigment."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/black"},{"term":"onyx","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A banded variety of chalcedony, a cryptocrystalline form of quartz.","A jet-black color, named after the gemstone."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Jet-black; often, glossily so."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/onyx"}],"entries":[{"title":"Onyx","blocks":[{"text":"Onyx is a typically black-and-white banded variety of agate, a silicate mineral. The bands can also be monochromatic with alternating light and dark bands. Sardonyx is a variety with red to brown bands alternated with black or white bands. The name \"onyx\" is also frequently used for level-banded (parallel-banded) agates, but in proper usage it refers to color pattern not band structure. Onyx, as a descriptive term, has also been incorrectly applied to parallel-banded varieties of alabaster, marble, calcite, obsidian, and opal, and misleadingly to materials with contorted banding, such as \"cave onyx\" and \"Mexican onyx\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Agate-Quartz-49959.jpg/330px-Agate-Quartz-49959.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onyx"},{"title":"Black","blocks":[{"text":"Black is a color that results from the absence or complete absorption of visible light. It is an achromatic color, without chroma, like white and grey. It is often used symbolically or figuratively to represent darkness. Black and white have often been used to describe opposites such as good and evil, the Dark Ages versus the Age of Enlightenment, and night versus day. Since the Middle Ages, black has been the symbolic color of solemnity and authority, and for this reason it is still commonly worn by judges and magistrates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png/330px-Tutankhamun_jackal_%28blacked%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black"},{"title":"Shades of black","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of black, or off-black colors, are colors that differ only slightly from pure black. These colors have a low lightness. From a photometric point of view, a color which differs slightly from black always has low relative luminance. Colors often considered \"shades of black\" include onyx, black olive, charcoal, and jet."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cc/Color_icon_black_new2.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_black"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Red_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg/500px-Red_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Red_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg/500px-Red_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cabochons of red onyx, also called sardonyx","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARed_onyx_-_Handicraft.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Black_Onyx.jpg/250px-Black_Onyx.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/14/Black_Onyx.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Black_Onyx.jpg/250px-Black_Onyx.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Black onyx with white streaks","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_Onyx.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg/500px-Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg/1280px-Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg/500px-Kunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Gemma Augustea is a Roman cameo produced 9–12 AD and carved in a two-layered onyx gem (19 × 23 cm).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKunsthistorisches_Museum_Vienna_June_2006_031.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Yellowstone-black-bear-07895.jpg/500px-Yellowstone-black-bear-07895.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Yellowstone-black-bear-07895.jpg/500px-Yellowstone-black-bear-07895.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYellowstone-black-bear-07895.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Queen_Victoria_after_Heinrich_von_Angeli.jpg/500px-Queen_Victoria_after_Heinrich_von_Angeli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Queen_Victoria_after_He